<h2>The Question Every Small Bakery Owner Asks</h2>
<p>You're opening a bakery. The lease is signed, the Convotherm is on order, and a commercial dishwasher quote just landed: $8,000 for a "small commercial undercounter." Meanwhile, a Bosch home dishwasher at the appliance store is $649. They both wash dishes with hot water. Why pay 12× more?</p>

<p>Three real reasons, in order of how badly they bite.</p>

<h2>1. The Temperature Compliance Problem</h2>
<p>The FDA Food Code section 4-501.112 (adopted by most US states) requires that <strong>mechanical warewashing equipment achieve a final rinse temperature of at least 180°F (82°C)</strong>, or alternatively achieve sanitization with an approved chemical sanitizer at specific concentrations. Residential dishwashers max out around 70°C (158°F) on the highest setting because home plumbing tankless heaters can't reliably deliver more, and the manufacturers don't certify for sanitization.</p>

