<h2>2. The Lifespan Problem</h2>
<p>A residential dishwasher is rated for roughly 1 cycle per day, 5 days per week, for 10 years — about 2,500 lifetime cycles. A commercial bakery runs 6–10 cycles per shift. That's 1,500–2,500 cycles in a single year. Translation: the home unit dies in 4–8 months instead of 10 years.</p>

<p>When it dies, what fails first is the heating element and the pump bearings — both not user-serviceable. You replace the whole machine. Two replacements per year × $400–$700 = $800–$1,400 annually. Across 5 years that's $4,000–$7,000 — already more than a commercial unit that lasts the full 10 years.</p>

<h2>3. The 600×400 Tray Problem</h2>
<p>If you operate a European-style bakery (Convotherm, Rational, Unox, MIWE ovens), your trays are 600×400 mm. <strong>Zero residential dishwashers accept that size.</strong> Their racks are designed for plates and bowls, max width ~500 mm. So in addition to compliance and lifespan issues, you physically cannot wash your primary bake-tray inventory.</p>

<p>The <a href="/product/">V-TAI JD-3</a> has a 650×550 mm rack specifically sized to accept 600×400 trays with clearance — the only machine in its price class that does. <a href="/what-it-washes/baking-trays-600x400/">See the full compatibility breakdown</a>.</p>

